\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The logarithmic derivative of a heat trace}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Let #{G} be a finite undirected graph without loops. Its
combinatorial Laplacian acts on functions on the vertex set by
##{(Lf)(v)=\sum_{w\sim v}\bigl(f(v)-f(w)\bigr),}
summed over the neighbors of #{v}. \ref{fdip-0002} permits an
empty indexing set. On the empty graph the function space is
the zero space, the Laplacian is defined, and
#{\trace(\ue^{-tL})=0}. For a graph with vertices,
the eigenvalues #{\lambda_j} are real and nonnegative, and the
multiplicity-counted spectral sum is
##{Z(t)=\sum_j \ue^{-t\lambda_j}.}
That sum equals #{\trace(\ue^{-tL})}. Both the
trace and the spectral sum remain defined for the empty graph,
where each is #{0}.}

\p{The logarithm of #{Z} is not defined when the trace is
#{0}, so the quotient below requires a nonempty graph.
Disconnected graphs and graphs with one vertex remain
included, and connectedness is not assumed. For #{t>0} and
such a nonempty #{G}, the logarithmic derivative is
##{d_s(t)=-2\frac{d\log Z}{d\log t}
=2t\frac{\sum_j\lambda_j \ue^{-t\lambda_j}}{Z(t)}.}
The function #{d_s} is the \newvocab{spectral dimension} of
#{G}. \ref{fdip-00CQ} inherits this nonempty requirement for
its logarithmic dimensions. No extension of #{d_s} to the
empty graph is asserted, and connectedness is not assumed.}

\p{A sequence of nonempty graphs of this kind has a
\newvocab{plateau} at a real number #{n\ge 0} when there are
windows #{0<a_k<b_k} with #{b_k/a_k\to\infty} and
##{\sup_{a_k\le t\le b_k}\lvert d_s^{(k)}(t)-n\rvert
\longrightarrow 0.}}
